## Run these

On either VM:
```bash
sudo grep -rn DocumentRoot /etc/apache2/sites-available/
sudo find / -xdev -name .htaccess 2>/dev/null
curl -s -H 'Metadata-Flavor: Google' http://metadata.google.internal/computeMetadata/v1/instance/image
curl -s -H 'Metadata-Flavor: Google' http://metadata.google.internal/computeMetadata/v1/instance/attributes/created-by
```

The metadata calls show the image and MIG each VM came from. If both VMs show the same image family, "same Apache version across instances" is well supported.

Optional full check of every non-Windows CVE in the report, on the frontend:
```bash
for c in CVE-2026-33007 CVE-2026-33006 CVE-2026-33857 CVE-2026-58415 CVE-2026-79768 CVE-2026-29170 CVE-2026-43951 CVE-2025-65082 CVE-2026-63718 CVE-2026-63292 CVE-2026-29168 CVE-2026-63045 CVE-2026-42536 CVE-2026-93546 CVE-2026-56153 CVE-2026-44186 CVE-2026-56449 CVE-2025-58098 CVE-2026-28780 CVE-2026-42535 CVE-2026-29167 CVE-2026-29169 CVE-2026-73636 CVE-2026-73637 CVE-2026-48005 CVE-2026-63686 CVE-2025-55753 CVE-2025-49630; do
  grep -q "$c" /tmp/apache2.changelog && echo "IN-LOG $c" || echo "NOT-IN-LOG $c"
done
```
`NOT-IN-LOG` is expected for CVEs in unloaded modules. It is only a problem if the CVE's module is loaded (core, ssl, proxy, proxy_http, http2, rewrite, headers, mime, env).

